WEBVTT

00:02:11.196 --> 00:02:11.696
in the morning.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:14.172
When I go to sleep at night, I think of you.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.840
You're like the air which surrounds me.

00:02:18.812 --> 00:02:21.732
The sky which spreads above me, the earth beneath my feet.

00:02:23.356 --> 00:02:24.016
When I hear music.

00:02:24.828 --> 00:02:27.528
When I see beauty, when I breathe in the sunlight, I think of you.

00:02:28.444 --> 00:02:29.704
You are all the women in my life.

00:02:30.732 --> 00:02:32.072
You were the sister I never had.

00:02:33.036 --> 00:02:34.396
The mother I have almost forgotten.

00:02:36.268 --> 00:02:38.268
The wife I have always dreamed of.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.600
isn't a relationship you can name which exists between a man and a woman of which I wouldn't say

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:49.320
let it be you.

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:52.464
to you.

00:02:56.044 --> 00:02:58.044
Okay, that's the second act curtain.

00:02:58.316 --> 00:02:59.996
I like that tempo, Lester. Keep it.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.120
Stick a pin in it.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:01.640
All right.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:02.800
Let's take a 10-minute break.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:04.110
Freddy, will you check on the...

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:04.510
Listen to this.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.740
The rumor along Shubert Alley is that Myra Hudson, the San Francisco heiress, who needs

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.980
another hit the way Rockefeller needs another million, is rumored to have her best play

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:14.980
yet in half a century.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.300
which Scott Martindale put into rehearsal this week.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.400
I wish they'd forget the heiress business.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.500
Just give your money to me, dear, and they'll stop bothering you.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.500
Do you want to talk about those second act changes now?

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.870
I think we ought to talk about this act.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.000
He's a real find, isn't he?

00:03:32.588 --> 00:03:34.288
And what a lovely speaking voice.

00:03:34.388 --> 00:03:36.748
He sounds romantic enough, just doesn't look romantic.

00:03:36.848 --> 00:03:38.348
He's an excellent actor, Myra.

00:03:38.448 --> 00:03:39.228
I know, Bill.

00:03:40.204 --> 00:03:41.604
But that isn't enough for this role.

00:03:42.348 --> 00:03:49.348
We've got to convince the audience that this character could recite three blind mice to Laura and she would think it was the most romantic poem in the world.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:54.516
He has to be the kind of charm boy that makes every woman in the audience sit right up and go, hmm.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.180
The instant he walks on that stage.

00:03:57.292 --> 00:03:59.732
I haven't seen an actor like that since John Barrymore.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.640
I've watched him from every section of this theater and I'm sorry, but he's just not right for the part

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.230
Have you decided to set yourself up as a judge as to who is romantic looking Myra?

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.750
I have to direct this play. In my opinion, he'd be great for the part

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:18.130
You're both entitled to your opinions, but I still say that he's not my idea of a romantic leading man

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:22.450
Sweetie, I'm only putting a paltry $85,000 in this play

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:27.610
I know that's only the interest on your securities, but it happens to be my money

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.470
That you made on my play

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.670
Well, there's no use in arguing.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:38.820
And I hate to break the news to Blaine.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.060
Well, tell him the truth.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.340
Make me the heavy.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.500
Say I exercise the dramatist privilege of cast approval.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:46.360
You heard her, Bill.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:47.580
You fire him.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.150
Then they wonder why I haven't a friend along Broadway.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.870
That's all for today, everybody.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:59.960
Oh, Mr. Blaine, can I see you on...

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.470
Ann, anything in the morning mail that needs an immediate answer?

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.710
Telegram from San Francisco from Steve Conning.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.390
Charles Hoyt phoned about an interview,

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:13.210
and the dramatist Gill asked if you can attend a committee meeting Saturday evening.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.270
Friday at 2 for Mr. Hoyt.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:17.690
Tell the Guild I'm in rehearsal, but I'll try.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.190
Wire, Steve, that the play opens on the 21st,

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.410
and I'll take the train to San Francisco on the following Monday.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.380
In your own native city of San Francisco,

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.460
there's an art gallery in the Legion of Honor

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.080
in which there's an oil painting of Casanova.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.620
It's quite obvious that you've never seen this painting.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.560
For your information, Miss Hudson,

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.120
this is what Casanova looked like.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.200
He had big ears, a scar over one eye,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.940
a broken nose, and a wart on his chin.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:47.930
Right here.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.210
I suggest, Miss Hudson, that when you return to San Francisco,

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:52.950
you visit this gallery and see this painting.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.480
Mr. Blaine. Mr. Blaine!

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.970
It's a true car. I'll be waiting for you at the Oakland Pier bar.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.240
Thank you, Ann. I'm so tired I don't want to see or talk to anyone

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.980
till I get to San Francisco. I'll take a rescuer on the train.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.680
I'll drive Anne to the airport tonight.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:32.260
Thanks, Bill.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:34.420
I'm like Mara. I'd rather ride the train.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:35.840
Have a wonderful trip.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:39.290
And if you have any spare time between here and Chicago,

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:40.450
write me another play.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.410
Oh, sure, sure. I'll have it done by Cleveland.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:43.470
Goodbye.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:44.030
Bye.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.910
Mr. Blaine. Oh, Mr. Blaine.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.610
Oh. Hello, Miss Hudson.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:49.870
Quite a coincidence you're getting on the train at Buffalo.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.750
Oh, I got on the train in New York, Miss Hudson.

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:54.010
As a matter of fact, I saw you embark.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:55.320
You did?

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.650
I saw the whole procession. Candy, books, flowers.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:07:59.850
Oh, now look.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.660
I thought perhaps you'd come down to say goodbye to me.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.980
And then I remembered you'd done that about a month ago.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.260
I tried to reach you several times about a month ago.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:10.640
Oh, did you? Yes.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.500
Excuse me, sir. Sorry.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.500
Now that you're here, won't you come in for a drink?

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:25.380
Shall I ring for the porter? Yes, please. Sit down.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:28.270
What are you reading?

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:32.360
Time out for crime.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:33.580
Yes, Miss Hudson.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:35.220
A couple of drinks. Scotch over ice, please.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:36.000
Yes, sir.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:37.910
How did you know?

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:40.970
What, your drink? I read the papers.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:44.820
Now that I've finally caught up with you,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.700
I'd like to really explain what really happened.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:47.220
Please don't.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.800
Look, Miss Hudson, you wrote an excellent play

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.240
and cast it according to your best judgment.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:54.080
I read the reviews. They were sensational. My congratulations.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.940
It's a little embarrassing coming from you.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.590
Forget it. Look.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.990
Let's get down to something far more important.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:04.120
Do you play stud poker?

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:06.500
I haven't for years.

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:08.390
You mean two-handed stud poker?

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:08.650
Yeah.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:11.820
I used to play like that with my father.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:13.680
Oh, we used to fight like that.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:14.480
Liked your father.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:15.940
I adored him.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:17.620
He was a great poker player.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:19.540
Are you still willing to take a chance?

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:20.770
I'll risk it.

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:21.510
All right, come in.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:23.710
Scotch over ice?

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.470
Oh, would you get us a deck of playing cards, please?

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:26.210
Yes, sir.

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.570
Always carry a deck on me in case a gentleman needs something or her.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:29.230
Well, thank you.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.260
Oh, no, you don't.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:32.500
Oh, please, I invited you.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:33.340
You should say not.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:34.240
But I insist.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:36.080
I'll tell you what.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:37.440
Let's play the match game.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:38.700
The what game?

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:39.320
The match game.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:39.740
Don't you know it?

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:41.260
I'm afraid I never heard of it.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.300
All right, I'll teach it to you.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:43.440
Excuse me.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.650
Do you have any book matches?

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:47.360
Yes, right here.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:48.150
Thank you.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:50.560
Now, this is how the game goes.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:52.210
Here are three matches for you.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:53.090
Let me have your hand.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.010
Three for you and three for me.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.940
Now, put both of your hands behind your back like this.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:09:59.980
Here.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.540
Put any number of matches, zero, one, two, or three, in one hand, close it into a fist,

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:09.320
and swing it out in front of you like this.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.350
Now, you guess how many matches there are in both our hands combined.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:14.210
In both?

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:14.650
Yes.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:16.770
Let's see, um...

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:17.320
Come on.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:18.300
Four.

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.340
Now, it's my turn to guess, and I say three.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:22.170
Now, turn your hand over like this.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:24.160
Four?

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:24.640
I won!

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:26.860
Yeah.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.250
Are you sure you haven't played this game before?

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:32.890
No, I'm positive.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:37.300
Thank you.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:38.140
Thank you, sir.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:39.850
Would you like me to close the door?

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:42.330
No, thank you.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:43.060
Yes, sir.

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.360
And now for a fast game of poker.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:46.980
We'll need some chips.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:48.520
You shuffle the cards.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:49.620
We'll use the matches for chips.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:59.220
All for you, me, for you, and for me.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:10:59.980
Don't you have one?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:22.940
one match too many sorry miss Hudson I'm entitled to a cut sorry cut and where

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:27.400
were you born near Pittsburgh my father was a coal miner I served a stretch

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:28.960
underground too as a kid I had to

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.940
Since then, I've traveled through Pennsylvania only at night.

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:33.620
I don't recognize the country in the daylight.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:37.710
My pop couldn't understand my wanting to breathe fresh air,

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:39.510
wear a clean face, read a book now and then.

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.730
When did you start to act?

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:45.680
In the army. Playing Lenny in Mice and Men in a camp show.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.120
A wonderful way to fight a war.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:50.080
But it was my downfall, too.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:53.070
Once you've lost your heart to the theater, it's hard to get it back.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.500
Besides, I won't take it back. I'm stubborn.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:11:59.840
I like people who make up their minds.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.170
and stick to it, whatever the odds.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:04.750
You're a very good actor, too.

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:10.930
You know, it wasn't my lack of faith in your ability as an actor that made me...

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.960
Please, it's been such a delightful breakfast, Miss Hudson.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:14.780
Let's keep it that way.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:16.870
Shall we play the match game?

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:18.590
Certainly not.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:20.460
We'll be in Chicago soon.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.740
I'm going to show you a part of Chicago you never dreamed existed.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.520
Sports, the theater, the Roman arena, all rolled into one.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.180
Can we see all of that in four hours?

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.980
We can see it in five minutes, where I'm going to take...

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:30.820
Let's go.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:39.130
You'll want to put your Chicago lipstick on.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.250
I'll wait for you in the lounge car.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:40.690
Fine.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:41.830
I'll meet you there in 10 minutes.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:42.090
Right.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.240
Doctor, how can I make arrangements to stay on this train

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.520
straight through to San Francisco?

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:54.480
My ticket's only good to Chicago.

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:56.980
Want to pull into the station?

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.420
I'll check to see if there's space available on the next pull.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:14.100
with the california zephyr i'd appreciate that yes feel that i'm halfway home when the train

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:23.480
leaves chicago must give you a warm feeling to have a home to go to it was such fun in chicago

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:29.980
today an acting school for wrestlers now i've seen everything it's all part of the theater that's

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:33.490
You work pretty hard at the theater, don't you?

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:34.940
Yes, I believe I do.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:36.160
Why do you work?

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:38.110
Why do I work?

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.810
I suppose it's a desire to achieve,

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:44.670
to earn my keep,

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:46.570
to stand on my own two feet

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:47.730
instead of my father's fortune,

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:50.450
and to make my own place in the world.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:52.300
Does that make sense to you?

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:53.470
Makes a lot of sense.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:55.280
Personal achievement.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.420
It's what we all want, each in our own way.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:13:59.980
Though, to be honest, if I'd inherit...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.740
and all that money, I'm not sure I'd have had the character.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:02.680
Of course you would.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.540
Look, a lot of little cells and glands and molecules get together.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.060
They spell out Lester Blaine.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:13.580
There's no other person in the whole world who exactly duplicates you.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.080
Two billion, one hundred and seventy-four million people.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:20.830
But only one Lester Blaine.

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.640
I'll travel part of the way with you on that idea.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:29.120
Two billion, one hundred and seventy-four million people in the world.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:31.920
Only one Myra Hudson.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:44.790
Excuse me.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:47.530
Your table for two in the dining car is ready, Mr. Blaine.

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:48.430
Thank you.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:51.560
Shall we?

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:58.980
These guys are painted with a thousand sparks.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:14:59.980
They are all...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.350
and every one doth shine, but there's but one in all doth hold his place.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.250
Julius Caesar, third act, scene two.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:08.820
Roger.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:10.450
I bet here's one you don't know.

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:16.080
Burn this night with torches, and know my heart's I hope well of tomorrow.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:16.980
Anthony.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:18.260
Right.

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:20.650
And tomorrow you'll be in San Francisco.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.760
Oh, you'll love my San Francisco.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:29.680
Is that a wedding, right?

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:33.720
my mother's. Wonderful.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:00.860
Who's that with her?

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:02.650
Why, it's that act.

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:03.200
Me.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:05.350
Myra, Myra, darling.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:05.610
Hi.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:06.710
Welcome home.

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:07.530
Good to see you.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:08.750
You look wonderful.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:10.300
You remember, Lester.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:12.140
Hello, Mr.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:16.270
And this is Steve Carney, my lawyer, my guide, and my dearest friend.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:16.970
Mr. Carney.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.260
Mr. Plain, ever been to San Francisco before?

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:20.460
Oh, very briefly.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:21.980
I came through with a camp show during the war.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.380
Oh, it's going to be such fun showing you San Francisco.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:26.530
How about dining and dancing tonight?

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:27.100
Tonight?

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.120
Well, aren't you tired from your trip, Myra?

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:29.900
Oh, not at all.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:31.160
Where shall we pick you up?

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:32.740
I'm not sure where I'm going to stay.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:35.860
Well, let's say the Fairmont at 9 o'clock, huh?

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:36.440
That'll be fine.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:47.670
They dance well together, don't they?

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:00.550
Thank you.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:36.470
Thank you. It's quite a cozy home.

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:39.470
Cozy enough for you to take your coat off.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:41.020
I'm delighted.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:48.610
Five o'clock already, and I'm not the least bit sleepy.

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:50.040
Is it that late?

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:52.810
It has been fun tonight, hasn't it?

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:54.000
It's still fun this morning.

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:04.970
This is where I work.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.460
Where your plays are born.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.360
Plays are born out of everything you've ever seen

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:11.820
and all the men and women you've ever known.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:13.260
This is where I put them on paper.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:15.240
Would you like a drink?

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:15.810
Yes.

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.450
Scotch, orange juice, milk?

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:19.400
Whatever you drink.

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:20.140
Milk, then.

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:24.520
What's this? A guided missile?

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:26.530
No, that's my dictating machine.

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:29.060
I've never seen one like it before.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:30.860
I had it built specially.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.340
I like to wander around when I dictate.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:35.380
I'll show you something.

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.110
See this? It's a microphone.

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:40.750
There are four others hidden around the room.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:42.660
There's the master switch over there.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.800
I turn it on, all the microphones are alive automatically.

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:48.980
When I start to talk, the disc records.

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:50.570
When I stop, it stops.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:52.330
Very ingenious.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:53.170
Skull.

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:57.770
Why don't you try it yourself?

00:18:59.630 --> 00:18:59.970
I'm walking...

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.680
Walk around the room. Say something.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:13.520
Well?

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:20.380
When I wake in the morning, when I go to sleep at night, I think of you.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:24.140
You're like the air which surrounds me,

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:28.320
the sky which spreads above me, the earth beneath my feet.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:36.140
When I hear music, when I see beauty, when I breathe in the sunlight, I think of you.

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:42.970
You are the sister I never had, the mother I have almost forgotten,

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:45.150
the wife I have always dreamed of.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:52.370
There isn't a relationship you can name which exists between a man and a woman

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:54.190
of which I wouldn't say let it be you.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:57.590
Let it be you.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:02.340
It's very flattering to be quoted.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:10.630
I'll play it back for you.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:24.120
Morning.

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:33.140
It surrounds me. It spreads a bit.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:11.480
Flattering to be quoted.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:21:59.980
was a

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:00.780
What a wonderful day.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:01.760
Every minute of it.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.500
Dinner's at seven. Don't be late, darling.

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:04.540
I'll be ahead of time.

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.420
Operator, I've been trying to get Prospect 17171 for two hours.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.000
Will you please check and make sure the phone isn't out of order.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:26.000
Thank you.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:56.710
We have a delegation of two to see what's happened to our hostess.

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:58.920
Myra, you must come down. I'll be there in a minute.

00:22:59.400 --> 00:22:59.980
You've been trying to...

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.740
He's been to teach me manners ever since I was a kid.

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:02.970
Everybody's waiting.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:11.740
Something must have happened to him.

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:13.440
Maybe the guy got his dates mixed.

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:14.340
You know, sometimes...

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.100
To you, I was giving this party just for him.

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:21.710
Myra, let me phone, so you can join your guests.

00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:47.850
Oh, he'd have called. He calls every night.

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:50.870
He wouldn't be two hours late, though, unless something had happened.

00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:52.750
I must go to him.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:55.190
Could I drive you, Myra?

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:57.650
No, thanks, Junior. This is something I have to do all by myself.

00:23:57.850 --> 00:23:58.870
What will I tell the guests?

00:23:59.050 --> 00:23:59.970
It doesn't matter. Tell them anything.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.080
Just tell them anything.

00:24:58.090 --> 00:24:59.910
I've been calling and calling. Why?

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:00.660
Why haven't you answered?

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:03.980
Forgive me.

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:12.330
Where are you going?

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:16.770
No place in your life, Myra.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:17.910
No place.

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:24.480
And you were leaving without even a word?

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:28.050
I don't belong to your world.

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:29.960
You are...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:40.210
so much I have nothing. Without you I have nothing.

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:35.130
Good morning.

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:36.310
Good morning.

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:39.260
How did you sleep last night?

00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:43.050
I wasn't asleep.

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:45.080
Come on, sleepyhead.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:45.780
No, don't.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:46.960
Don't do that to me.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:47.740
Where's your bathing suit?

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:48.920
Over there in the drawer.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:50.800
Which one is it?

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:51.800
That one.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:54.880
Come on, let's take a swim before breakfast.

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:55.480
All right.

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:57.640
Turn your head away.

00:26:59.540 --> 00:26:59.980
Go on, Tim.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:00.800
Turn your head away.

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:01.740
I'd like to look at you.

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:04.450
You couldn't possibly at this hour in the morning.

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:06.010
Oh, anybody can look at you in the afternoon.

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:08.180
But I haven't even got my lipstick on.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:09.880
A woman has to wear lipstick.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.300
I feel positively naked without it.

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:14.520
Darling.

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:21.030
Let's get out of here before I forget I'm a married man.

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:22.830
Do you want this, Diff?

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:23.690
Yes, please.

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:26.370
I'll fix breakfast for us after our swim.

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:29.020
Where are the steps of the float?

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:30.440
Right here.

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:32.200
Follow me.

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:32.760
Okay.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:37.740
Whoa!

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:42.280
What's the matter, darling?

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:44.110
It's a precipice.

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:48.690
I've been running up and down these steps ever since I was 12.

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:49.950
Don't you ever do it again.

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:50.510
Why not?

00:27:50.650 --> 00:27:51.750
Remember what Nietzsche said.

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:52.690
Live dangerously.

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:54.050
You know what happened to Nietzsche.

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:54.410
What?

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:55.340
He's dead.

00:27:57.740 --> 00:27:59.960
Mr. and Mrs. Lester Blaine will proceed on this...

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:01.420
a ski-jump at a nice, slow walk.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:02.190
All right.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:03.520
Watch out!

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:04.760
What is it?

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:06.740
There isn't even a car drill.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:08.970
There's no danger, not really.

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:09.470
Oh, no?

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:12.700
You'd get killed falling down these steps.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.180
What a beautiful place for us.

00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:18.770
You know what we do here in the wintertime?

00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:19.070
No.

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:19.510
We ski.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.300
Mrs. Blaine, you're about to be dumped.

00:28:22.460 --> 00:28:23.530
Oh, no, no, no!

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:23.930
Yes, you are!

00:28:56.880 --> 00:28:57.680
Dr. Van Roon.

00:28:58.250 --> 00:28:58.670
You do, doctor.

00:28:59.450 --> 00:28:59.970
Looks like you marry me.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.640
He's a good, healthy specimen, Myra.

00:29:01.860 --> 00:29:04.400
Oh, darling, I'm so happy for you.

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:05.360
I hope you play bridge.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:06.780
Very badly.

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:08.380
Fine, that's what we're looking for.

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:10.140
A bad bridge player with good money.

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:10.860
George!

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:12.180
Just a joke, kid.

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:13.400
Good evening, Mr. Connie.

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:13.980
Julius.

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:16.950
How have you been, Myra?

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:17.590
Fine.

00:29:18.010 --> 00:29:19.730
We had a divine time at the summer house.

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:23.600
Oh, Myra, did my mother come yet?

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:25.640
Yes, she's with Tom and Francesca.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:26.560
Oh, I see her.

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:28.240
Come on, darling, say hello to Mother.

00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:29.060
Yes, George.

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:29.920
Excuse me.

00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:30.800
Yes, Doctor.

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:31.260
Sure, Doctor.

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:31.980
Myra.

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:33.190
Hello, Junior.

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:34.640
Congratulations.

00:29:35.570 --> 00:29:36.090
Thank you.

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:38.150
This is Irene Neves from New York.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:38.870
Myra Hudson.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.320
No, it's Blaine now, isn't it?

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:42.020
Yes, it is.

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:43.430
How do you do, Miss Neves?

00:29:43.490 --> 00:29:44.670
How do you do, Mrs. Blaine?

00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:46.310
This is my husband, Lester Blaine.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:47.720
How do you do, Mr. Blaine?

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:48.360
Miss Neves.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:50.200
Welcome to California, Miss Neves.

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:50.720
Thank you.

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:52.780
Are you a visitor or are you going to stay here?

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.940
No, I intend to become a San Franciscan.

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:57.440
I've taken an apartment at the Tamil Pais.

00:29:57.770 --> 00:29:58.310
How nice.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:29:59.730
You'll excuse us, Myra.

00:29:59.730 --> 00:29:59.910
like

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:03.540
Junior, introduce Miss Neves to our guests and give her a drink, will you?

00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:03.900
Sure.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:05.960
It's very nice to have met you both.

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:09.400
Hello, Steve.

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:10.310
Hello, Junior.

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:12.670
This is Irene Neves, my brother Steve.

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:13.910
How do you do, Miss Neves?

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:15.610
How about you and I having a drink together?

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:21.140
Don't get out. It's too late.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:22.950
Thank you for a lovely evening.

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:26.830
I usually take my girls to the front door and kiss them goodnight.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:28.800
You stay right where you are.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:36.640
Good night, dear. Call me tomorrow.

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:38.340
Okay. Good night.

00:30:38.930 --> 00:30:39.770
Good night, Junior.

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:53.860
Can I give to one in San Francisco?

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:57.490
An old friend of mine married a San Francisco girl.

00:30:59.530 --> 00:30:59.730
Here.

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:03.080
I'll show you. It was in all the New York papers.

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:08.300
Good picture, both of them, isn't it?

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:09.200
Don't be cute.

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:12.050
I thought I'd come out here to see how you were getting along.

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:12.930
How long have you been here?

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:14.910
A week, and take your hands off me.

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:17.700
Where did you meet Carney?

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:22.870
At his office. You see, I thought I might need a lawyer.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:27.100
Your wife's lawyer from Choice.

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:28.760
Steve's my wife's lawyer.

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:29.270
Yes.

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:31.980
But Junior is more impressionable.

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:34.340
What have you said to impress him?

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:38.840
He said you were going to Chicago for a job.

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:40.140
What have you said to impress him?

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:45.500
You mean, have I told him why a certain woman lent you 5,000?

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:49.370
Have you? Have you?

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:50.740
Yet.

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:54.640
And I haven't told him about the house in Far Island either.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:57.200
Or the night after New Year's.

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:08.940
If you ever do, you're going to need a new face.

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:11.940
Remember that.

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:19.920
Thanks. Thanks a lot.

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:21.730
Thanks for what?

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:28.400
Still loving me.

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:43.070
I'll call back, and please hold the phone calls for a while, Ms. Carter.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:48.140
Now, as I understand it, this all boils down to a matter of dollars and cents.

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:49.170
That's right.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:52.680
You realize, of course, that your wife is a very wealthy woman.

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:53.800
I've never discussed it with her.

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:57.330
I see. Before I pursue the matter further,

00:32:57.490 --> 00:32:59.410
I think I should know just how much money you require.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:05.060
Naturally, I can't pretend to support Myra, but I ought to at least support myself.

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:07.380
Oh, certainly. I had no intention of implying that you're...

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:11.190
I'm sure of it. You're too good a friend of Myra's, and I hope eventually of mine.

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:13.420
Excuse me, Les.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:25.060
Yes? I'll talk to you about it later.

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:29.540
You were saying there's very little work for an actor here in San Francisco.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.480
Yes. Still, Myra comes first with me, so since her friends and her interests are here,

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:36.760
this is where we'll have to live.

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:38.680
I'm glad to hear you say that.

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:41.560
Now, if you could give me some advice on what kind of employment to look for.

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:46.200
I don't like to use my wife's connections, but unfortunately, I don't have any of my own.

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:51.720
See, you realize that with Myra's income as a playwright,

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:53.740
there's no real necessity for you to work at all.

00:33:54.790 --> 00:33:57.210
There's a very real necessity for my going to work, Steve.

00:33:58.420 --> 00:33:59.980
You see, I'm not the kind of a man who could live on...

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:03.690
I understand.

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:06.580
Say, Steve, before I prepare the conveyance...

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:07.880
Here's a friend of yours.

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:09.100
Oh, hello, Lester.

00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:09.780
Hello, Junior.

00:34:10.060 --> 00:34:10.660
Good to see you.

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:11.220
Thank you.

00:34:11.450 --> 00:34:14.370
Social visit, or is Carney and Carney about to get a new client?

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:15.510
Yes, he's right here.

00:34:15.930 --> 00:34:16.690
Telephone for you.

00:34:16.890 --> 00:34:18.310
Miss Irene Neves.

00:34:18.510 --> 00:34:18.650
Oh.

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:19.340
Junior.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:20.780
Pardon me, Lester.

00:34:21.140 --> 00:34:21.380
Sure.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:22.240
Hello, Irene.

00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:23.960
You're dead?

00:34:24.350 --> 00:34:24.610
Yes.

00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:26.560
I had a wonderful time, too.

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:29.350
How about trying it again sometime soon?

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:36.180
Tonight? Oh, don't worry. You couldn't get rid of me if you tried.

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:04.100
What a charming place you chose for a rendezvous.

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:06.340
You're still running after young Carney. What's the idea?

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:07.380
You're hurting me.

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:09.600
I haven't started to hurt you unless you give me some straight answers.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:10.640
Why are you seeing Carney?

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.040
Because you haven't called me in eight days,

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:14.220
because of the rent stew,

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:16.380
and because I'd rather eat dinner than starve.

00:35:17.430 --> 00:35:19.610
I'll get you some money as soon as I can. I've got to watch my step.

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:21.510
And what am I supposed to do while you watch it?

00:35:21.630 --> 00:35:23.290
Look at the walls and wait for the phone to ring?

00:35:24.910 --> 00:35:26.490
I'll tell you what you can do. You can help me.

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:29.960
Junior likes you. He talk easy for...

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.820
somebody he liked. What do you want him to talk about? My wife is giving a lot of money away to

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:40.160
a thing called the Hudson Hart Foundation. I want to know who gets how much and who handles the

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:44.900
money. Well, why don't you turn over in bed some morning and ask her? Why don't we really start

00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:52.750
a work together the way we used to? Yes, why don't we? See what you can get out of Carnie?

00:35:56.720 --> 00:35:58.480
I'll turn him inside out.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.000
Thanks for watching!

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:52.480
It took five martinis. He really talked last night.

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:56.910
You want to know what part of her father's estate she's giving away?

00:36:57.980 --> 00:36:58.700
All of it.

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:03.730
I wish I was kidding, but according to Junior,

